While these changes will fix the issue reported in #46048, it however only fixes a subset of  issues related to parsing invalid TAP content. Users can console.log any invalid TAP. 

For instance, here are a few examples of invalid content that will make the parser throw or break:

```javascript
const test = require("node:test");

test('test 1');

test('testing', () => {
  console.log(-123);
  console.log(123);
  console.log('-123');
  console.log('123');
  console.log('a 123');
  console.log(123, '123');
  console.log('123', 123);
  console.log('123', 'a');
  console.log('123 abc');
  console.log('abc 123');

  console.log('1.....4');

  console.log('TAP 12');
  console.log('TAP version v14');
  console.log('TAP TAP TAP');

  console.log('--- yaml');
  console.log('... ... yaml');
  console.log('ook 1');
  console.log('   ok 98'); // 3 spaces indent
  console.log('### dd');

  console.log('pragma ++++++');

  console.log('Bailout!');
});

test('test 3');
```
So, I sugest the following fix that will allow us to catch as many bugs as possible that are related to parsing invalid TAP.

**The trick is to allow the parser to throw exceptions even in async parsing mode, and then catch those excetption (both invalid tap exception and runtime exceptions), and show them as comments to user.** This way we won't have to change each parsing rule to catch errors.

